# Test Timeout Conventions

## Critical Rule: Keep Test Timeouts Short

**Maximum timeout: 1 second (1000ms)** for any test operation.

## Why Short Timeouts Matter

Long timeouts significantly slow down the entire test suite:
- Tests with 5-10 second timeouts make the suite unbearably slow
- Fast feedback is essential for development workflow
- CI/CD pipelines should complete quickly

## Timeout Guidelines

### ✅ DO: Use Short Timeouts

```typescript
// Good: Maximum 1 second timeout
await waitFor(
    () => {
        expect(mockFunction).toHaveBeenCalled();
    },
    { timeout: 1000 },
);

// Good: No timeout specified (uses default ~1s)
await waitFor(() => {
    expect(result.current.isLoading).toBe(false);
});
```

### ❌ DON'T: Use Long Timeouts

```typescript
// Bad: 10 second timeout
it(
    "test name",
    async () => {
        // test code
    },
    { timeout: 10000 }, // ❌ Too slow!
);

// Bad: 5 second waitFor timeout
await waitFor(
    () => {
        expect(value).toBe(true);
    },
    { timeout: 5000 }, // ❌ Too slow!
);
```

## Debounce Testing Strategy

**Best Practice: Use Fake Timers** instead of real timeouts for debounced behavior:

```typescript
it("debounces search input", async () => {
    vi.useFakeTimers(); // ✅ Use fake timers

    const { result } = renderHook(() => useSearchHook());

    act(() => {
        result.current.setSearch("test");
    });

    // Fast-forward time instead of waiting
    act(() => {
        vi.advanceTimersByTime(500); // ✅ Instant, no real waiting
    });

    await waitFor(() => {
        expect(mockFetch).toHaveBeenCalledWith("test");
    });

    vi.useRealTimers(); // Clean up
});
```

**Avoid:** Real timeouts even if short:
```typescript
// ❌ Bad: Actually waits 1 second
await waitFor(
    () => {
        expect(mockFetch).toHaveBeenCalledWith("test");
    },
    { timeout: 1000 },
);

// ❌ Bad: Actually waits 100ms
await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 100));
```

## Async Operation Testing

For async operations like API calls:

```typescript
it("fetches data", async () => {
    const mockFetch = vi.fn(async () => ({ data: [] }));

    const { result } = renderHook(() => useDataFetch(mockFetch));

    // Wait for loading to complete
    await waitFor(() => {
        expect(result.current.isLoading).toBe(false);
    }); // Uses default ~1s timeout

    expect(mockFetch).toHaveBeenCalled();
});
```

## Fake Timers for Time-Based Tests

**Always use fake timers** for testing debounce, throttle, or any time-based behavior:

```typescript
it("handles debounced search", async () => {
    vi.useFakeTimers(); // ✅ Enable fake timers

    const { result } = renderHook(() => useDebounceHook());

    // Make changes
    act(() => {
        result.current.setValue("a");
        vi.advanceTimersByTime(100); // Partial time
    });

    act(() => {
        result.current.setValue("ab");
        vi.advanceTimersByTime(100); // More partial time
    });

    act(() => {
        result.current.setValue("abc");
        vi.advanceTimersByTime(500); // Complete the debounce
    });

    // Assertions
    await waitFor(() => {
        expect(mockFn).toHaveBeenCalledWith("abc");
    });

    vi.useRealTimers(); // ✅ Always clean up
});
```

### Benefits of Fake Timers
- ✅ **Instant**: Tests run in milliseconds, not seconds
- ✅ **Deterministic**: No timing race conditions
- ✅ **Precise**: Control exact timing of events
- ✅ **No actual waiting**: CPU can focus on other tests

### When NOT to Use Fake Timers
- Real API calls (use mocks instead)
- Animation testing requiring visual verification
- Integration tests with external services

## Vitest Configuration

Default timeout is set in `vitest.config.ts`:

```typescript
export default defineConfig({
    test: {
        testTimeout: 5000, // Overall test timeout
        hookTimeout: 10000, // Setup/teardown timeout
    },
});
```

Individual test timeouts should be much shorter (≤1000ms).

## Common Patterns

### ✅ Fast Test Pattern

```typescript
describe("MyComponent", () => {
    it("renders correctly", () => {
        render(<MyComponent />);
        expect(screen.getByText("Hello")).toBeInTheDocument();
    });

    it("handles click", async () => {
        const onClick = vi.fn();
        render(<MyComponent onClick={onClick} />);

        await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole("button"));

        expect(onClick).toHaveBeenCalled();
    });
});
```

### ❌ Slow Test Pattern (Avoid)

```typescript
describe("MyComponent", () => {
    it(
        "waits for slow operation",
        async () => {
            // Don't do this!
            await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 3000));
        },
        { timeout: 5000 },
    );
});
```

## Summary

- **Use fake timers for time-based tests** (`vi.useFakeTimers()` + `vi.advanceTimersByTime()`)
- **Avoid explicit timeouts** - use `waitFor` without timeout parameter when possible
- **Maximum timeout: 1000ms (1 second)** if absolutely necessary
- **No `setTimeout` or `new Promise` with delays** in tests
- Mock slow operations instead of waiting for them
- Fast tests = better developer experience
- Keep the test suite under 30 seconds total runtime

### Quick Reference
```typescript
// ✅ BEST: Use fake timers
vi.useFakeTimers();
vi.advanceTimersByTime(500);
vi.useRealTimers();

// ✅ GOOD: No timeout specified (fast default)
await waitFor(() => expect(x).toBe(y));

// ⚠️ OK: Short timeout if needed
await waitFor(() => expect(x).toBe(y), { timeout: 1000 });

// ❌ BAD: Long timeout
await waitFor(() => expect(x).toBe(y), { timeout: 5000 });

// ❌ BAD: Real waiting
await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 500));
```

